---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { personLd, breadcrumbLd } from '../data/jsonld';
import headshot from '../assets/coldie-headshot.jpg';

// Person JSON-LD with the headshot attached (helps answer engines + rich results).
const person = personLd();
person.image = new URL(headshot.src, site.url).href;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={site.oneLineBio}
  path="/about"
  jsonld={[person, breadcrumbLd([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about' }])]}
>
  <article class="about">
    <div class="wrap about__wrap">
      <div class="about__intro">
        <figure class="about__portrait">
          <Image
            src={headshot}
            alt="Coldie, wearing red/cyan 3D glasses and a Sub Pop cap — black-and-white portrait."
            widths={[360, 720]}
            sizes="(max-width: 720px) 60vw, 320px"
            loading="eager"
          />
          <figcaption class="about__credit muted small">Photo: Matthew Davis</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="about__lead">
          <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
          <h1>Coldie</h1>
          <!-- Answer-first: a self-contained factual sentence engines can lift whole. -->
          <p class="lede">{site.oneLineBio}</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Artist statement</h2>
      <blockquote class="about__statement">
        <p>
          I work in depth — the space just in front of and just behind the flat surface, where the eye is
          fooled into believing an image is no longer flat. For more than fifteen years I’ve pursued that
          illusion across mediums: stereoscopic and lenticular prints, anaglyph 3D, kinetic sculpture,
          photography, and on-chain editions. My portraits are built by recombining many source images into
          one, the way a person is built from many influences. The tools change — a hand-built 3D camera, a
          lenticular press, a blockchain, a VR headset — but the work is always the same: take something
          flat, and give it dimension.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>The auction arc</h2>
      <p>
        Coldie’s work has appeared at all three major auction houses — and each time, the houses chose the
        3D work. In 2022, Sotheby’s included his stereoscopic art in <em>Inside the World of MaxStealth</em>,
        the first single-owner NFT auction held live in its salesroom, alongside Beeple, XCOPY, Pak, and
        Hackatao — the last a collaborator on the 3D work <em>UAP — Unidentified Art Phenomenon</em>. In 2021,
        Bonhams featured <em>Proof of Work — Genesis</em> in its SuperRare “CryptOGs” sale. In 2024, Christie’s
        offered <em>Warren Buffett — Filthy Fiat</em>, the debut of his portraiture as physical 3D sculpture.
      </p>

      <h2>Front Row 3D — concert photography (2009–2018)</h2>
      <p>
        For nearly a decade, Coldie photographed live music in stereoscopic 3D using a camera of his own
        construction, serving as the official 3D photographer of the Coachella Festival in 2009 and 2010. He
        shot believing a technology that did not yet exist would one day render the images in full
        dimensional color — and in 2021, viewing the archive in VR for the first time, that conviction was
        confirmed. The series, exhibited as <em>Front Row 3D</em> at Gazelli Art House, London, now stands as
        a rare dimensional record of performers including Chris Cornell, Mac Miller, and DMX.
      </p>

      <h2>Credentials</h2>
      <p>
        Work selected for national juried exhibitions: 2016, juried by Jenny Gheith, Assistant Curator of
        Painting and Sculpture, SFMOMA (now Curator and Interim Head of Painting and Sculpture); 2018, juried
        by LA critic and curator Mat Gleason. Exhibited at Gazelli Art House, London. First stereoscopic
        artwork minted on-chain (2018). Work brought to auction at all three major houses — Christie’s,
        Sotheby’s, and Bonhams. Represented at Eterno Gallery, Lisbon. The 2016 and 2018 juried pieces were
        anaglyph 3D landscapes on canvas, shown with 3D glasses on display.
      </p>

      <p class="about__back"><a class="link-arrow" href="/#studio">See the work</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) var(--section-y); }
  .about__wrap { max-width: calc(72ch + 2 * var(--gutter)); }
  .about__intro {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 320px 1fr;
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }
  .about__portrait { margin: 0; }
  .about__portrait img {
    width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 1; object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--radius); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--bg-raise);
  }
  .about__credit { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .about__lead { min-width: 0; }
  .about__lead h1 { margin: 0.1em 0 0.4em; }
  .about__lead .lede { margin: 0; }
  .about h1 { margin: 0.2em 0 0.5em; }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .about__intro { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .about__portrait { max-width: 260px; }
  }
  .about h2 { margin-top: 2.2em; }
  .about__statement { margin: 1.5em 0; padding-left: 1.2em; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); }
  .about__statement p { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 1.2rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }
  .about__back { margin-top: 3rem; }
</style>
